{"id":"transcript-stream-quadratic","parent":"perfc-server","ord":"a3","title":"Chat streaming is quadratic in bytes AND stop-and-wait (one frame per round trip) — a 15 kB answer costs more wire than the whole app","done":"2026-08-22T12:48:48-04:00","desc":"**Symptom:** chat answers stream in slow, lumpy pulses from kolu-bot (India) to a laptop (Canada) over a DIRECT tailnet (~250ms RTT; DERP ruled out via `tailscale status`).\\n\\n**Measured two ways — ACP chunk counts on kolu-bot, and a full browser+websocket instrument over an artificial delayed TCP link (scratchpad: chatwire.ts, proxy.ts, stream-agent.ts). Two independent defects:**\\n\\n**1. Quadratic bytes (every agent, every link).** The chat rebuilds the whole accumulated string and upserts the FULL row per chunk (transcript.ts:428 `#grow` → runtime.ts:1067 upsert), and kolu's coalescer is a `queueMicrotask` latch (kolu server.ts:713) that merges nothing because ACP chunks arrive on separate I/O ticks — one frame per chunk, each carrying the entire answer so far. Frame k ≈ k·chunk + ~190B envelope. Amplification ≈ chunks/2:\\n- opencode on kolu-bot: 3.2 kB answer → 643 chunks (~5B each) → **1.04 MB on the wire, 323×.**\\n- synthetic 800-chunk answer (37.9 kB) → **15.0 MB, 406×.**\\n- real Claude adapter, 14.6 kB answer → 843 kB — **more wire than loading the ENTIRE app** (HTML+JS+fonts+icons = 777 kB the same session).\\n\\n**2. Stop-and-wait: exactly one frame per round trip (unexpected, bigger on a long link).** The transcript stream ACKs each frame with a hard-coded credit window of 1 (effect RpcServer/RpcClient `supportsAck`), and kolu builds streaming members with `Stream.fromAsyncIterable` (kolu server.ts:372,409) — the NON-coalescing Effect branch — so each round trip carries one delta, never the batch accumulated during it. Time = chunks × RTT, independent of bandwidth (capping the link to 5 Mbps cost 8%; it ran 93% idle). At RTT 200ms, a 400-chunk answer the agent finished in 10.6s took **82.6s to finish arriving — the panel 72s behind, painting at 5 chunks/s.** 800 chunks: 147s behind.\\n- **When it bites:** any agent streaming faster than ~5 chunks/s at 200ms RTT. The pinned Claude adapter batches (~1.4 chunks/s) so it stays hidden today — the long-haul Claude run showed ZERO lag. opencode and every token-level streamer land at 20–40 chunks/s and hit the wall immediately, which is exactly the human's report.\\n\\n**Fix, cheapest first (the measurement ranks them):**\\n1. Throttle transcript.say / chat.ts:434 so chunks in one tick collapse before publish — kolu's existing microtask coalescer then genuinely merges them. Fixes BOTH defects, no protocol change. Start here.\\n2. Give the row an append representation (a chunk delta carries only its new text) — fixes bytes to O(N), independent of (1).\\n3. Make the transcript member produce via the Effect/Queue shape instead of `Stream.fromAsyncIterable` so a round trip carries the whole accumulated batch — fixes the RTT stall; a kolu-side change.\\n- Browser appends and throttles the Markdown re-render to match.\\n\\n**Not this node:** kimi-k3 via LiteLLM from India was also slow at the model level (first token 7.3s) — provider latency, separate from the wire.","created":"2026-08-22T08:39:49-04:00","changed":"2026-08-22T12:48:48-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human 2026-08-22 (slow streaming, Canada↔India direct tailnet); measured on kolu-bot","kolu-pr":"fix 3 = juspay/kolu#2202 (re-land of #2199 with a bounded Stream.buffer(512, suspend) + settle-based membership tests), MERGED 2026-08-22 12:36 as 020f05af with all ci:: contexts green on both platforms (history: #2199 merged 8b9820ed without odu — orchestrator error — found red, reverted via #2201 = 9a1137b8); olai's pin moves to 020f05af in #345","pr":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/345 — merged 2026-08-22 as d6f80e78 (fixes 1+2, pin to kolu 020f05af) + https://github.com/juspay/kolu/pull/2202 — merged as 020f05af (fix 3). Measured: 1,118 kB/643 frames → 19.6 kB/82 loopback; 1,093 kB → 18.6 kB over a 200 ms relay. The panel-behind number was not reproduced by the lane's relay instrument (0.2 s behind before) — verify on the real Canada↔India link after deploy."}}
{"id":"perf-sweep-2026-08-24","ord":"aE","title":"The second O(n) sweep: what one keystroke still costs (2026-08-24)","desc":"A fresh whole-codebase audit (four parallel Opus reviewers, 2026-08-24), looking for work that grows with the whole vault (or the whole conversation) but is paid on every small event — a keystroke, a write, a stream frame, a tool call. The 2026-08-19 sweep's items were excluded and separately re-verified: three still true, one reworded (list_documents).\n\nThe one-line summary: **the browser is now clean — the server took over the walking.** The vault-in-browser series really did kill the client-side vault walks; what's left in the browser is page-sized. The heavy costs now live in the server's per-tab recomputation, in git-per-write, in the model's date/tag bookkeeping, and in a new family of chat-stream quadratics.\n\nEach child group is one area; each leaf is one standing cost with the evidence and a fix shape. Worst first within each group. Rough overall ranking: streams-per-tab and git-per-write dominate everything; the chat tool quadratic is what a long agent session feels; the agenda/tag costs grow with the vault's age.","see":["perf-sweep-2026-08-19","search-index"],"created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00","custom":{"from":"sweep by four parallel Opus subagents over format, server+surface, ops+chat, web client; findings verified per-event with file:line evidence"}}
{"id":"psw2-server","parent":"perf-sweep-2026-08-24","ord":"a0","title":"Server: every open tab pays for every keystroke","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00"}
{"id":"perf-streams-per-tab","parent":"psw2-server","ord":"a0","title":"Five page answers are rebuilt on every write — once per open tab","todo":true,"desc":"The page, filter, calendar, overdue and move-picker answers are each wired as a poll stream, and the framework runs one full recompute loop PER SUBSCRIBED TAB, fired by every write (runtime.ts:967-1020). The \"did anything change?\" comparison runs AFTER the rebuild — it saves the network frame, never the work — and each read also spins a fresh fiber and, on the nothing-changed path, still walks the whole answer structurally to say so. So three tabs on the same page = three identical rebuilds per keystroke.\n\nFix shape: compute once per revision per distinct question and share the result across subscribers; find a cheap \"could this have changed?\" test that runs before the rebuild, not after.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00"}
{"id":"perf-git-per-write","parent":"psw2-server","ord":"a1","title":"Git is consulted about every uncommitted file on every write — and the bill grows until you commit","todo":true,"desc":"Every revision runs `git status`, and then for EVERY dirty outline a `git show HEAD:<file>` subprocess plus a full parse of the old copy, to build the node-level diff (runtime.ts:617, pending.ts:800-860). Under manual commit the dirty list only grows during a session, so typing literally gets slower the longer a commit is deferred. The comment admits only \"one git status on a clean directory\".\n\nFix shape: HEAD's copy of a file cannot change between commits — cache the parsed old copy per (commit, file); or compute the detailed diff only when the Commit UI actually asks for it.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00"}
{"id":"perf-narrowing-double-build","parent":"psw2-server","ord":"a2","title":"A tab with a filter open builds its page twice per write","todo":true,"desc":"The narrowing stream re-runs the exact same page construction the page stream just finished, and re-parses the filter text from scratch each time (runtime.ts:1007 → ops query.ts:416 → narrowingOf calling shownOf again). The comment claims \"the walk is the page's\" — it is a second page's.\n\nFix shape: derive the narrowing from the page answer that already exists; parse the filter once per text change, not once per revision.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00"}
{"id":"perf-connect-firstframe","parent":"psw2-server","ord":"a3","title":"Opening or re-opening a tab ships the whole heads map and a full transcript copy","todo":true,"desc":"Each connect/reconnect encodes one entry per served file (runtime.ts:890) and copies the entire chat transcript and live pieces (runtime.ts:898, 928). Fine for one tab; a server restart with many tabs open is a reconnect storm paying O(tabs × files). Mild — listed so it is written down.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00"}
{"id":"psw2-model","parent":"perf-sweep-2026-08-24","ord":"a1","title":"The model: costs that grow with the vault's age","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00"}
{"id":"perf-agenda-history-walk","parent":"psw2-model","ord":"a0","title":"The overdue count walks every past day — and fully dresses every overdue item — to produce two numbers","todo":true,"desc":"behind() visits every day before today and builds the complete entry for each overdue node (parent trail, blockers, progress) even though the subscriber only wants two counts; recomputed per revision (agenda.ts:348-399, ops query.ts:588). The forward walk and the calendar likewise skip LINEARLY over every earlier day instead of jumping to the start (agenda.ts:398, dates.ts:92). A vault with years of journal days pays for all of them on every write.\n\nFix shape: maintain the two counts as an index the patcher keeps (the dated-index precedent), and start day-walks with a sorted lookup instead of a skip.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00"}
{"id":"perf-key-resort","parent":"psw2-model","ord":"a1","title":"Editing near a popular tag re-sorts that tag's whole vault-wide list","todo":true,"desc":"When a patch touches a key, the patcher rebuilds that key's ENTIRE member list — copy plus re-sort — even though only a few members moved (patch.ts:380-388). Typing in any file that mentions #kitchen re-sorts every #kitchen record in the vault; same for a busy day in byDay or a large parent's children. The header's \"bounded by the touched file\" claim covers the folds above this line, not this rebuild.\n\nFix shape: splice into the already-sorted list instead of copying and re-sorting it.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00"}
{"id":"perf-overlay-copies","parent":"psw2-model","ord":"a2","title":"The patcher copies its carried bookkeeping even when a patch doesn't touch it","todo":true,"desc":"Each layered index deep-copies its carried layer in the constructor — three copies — and then throws them away untouched if the patch wrote nothing to that index (overlay.ts:280-333). The layer may grow to half the corpus before flattening, so a long editing session pays up to 3 × n/2 copies × 6 indexes per keystroke for nothing.\n\nFix shape: copy lazily, on the first actual write to the layer.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00"}
{"id":"perf-doc-backlinks-index","parent":"psw2-model","ord":"a3","title":"A document's page re-scans every link in every file, on every write","todo":true,"desc":"\"What links to this document?\" has no reverse index — referrersTo loops over every face in the directory and tests every link (backlinks.ts:242-275), per revision, per tab sitting on any .md/.html page. The node-to-node direction next door IS indexed; this is the one direction that isn't, and the comment admits it.\n\nFix shape: one more maintained index, links → documents, kept by the patcher like the others.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00"}
{"id":"perf-move-picker-dfs","parent":"psw2-model","ord":"a4","title":"The move picker re-walks the moved branch once per candidate, per keystroke","todo":true,"desc":"For each destination in the picker's result batch, movingOf re-runs an identical depth-first walk of everything the moved row draws, just to ask \"would this create a loop?\" (moving.ts:194, 247). The walks differ only in the destination being looked for.\n\nFix shape: walk once from the moved row, collect the reachable set, answer the whole batch against it.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00"}
{"id":"perf-filename-conventions","parent":"psw2-model","ord":"a5","title":"Pins and the inbox re-derive their by-filename answers on every write","todo":true,"desc":"Every revision re-allocates the full file list, lowercases every basename and re-sorts, for answers that only change when a file is added, removed or renamed (shelf.ts:192, inbox.ts:99, runtime.ts:671-716). The cell equality swallows the frame, not the work.\n\nFix shape: recompute only when the set of file paths actually changed.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00"}
{"id":"psw2-chat","parent":"perf-sweep-2026-08-24","ord":"a2","title":"Chat: streams that slow down as the conversation grows","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00"}
{"id":"perf-tool-stream-quadratic","parent":"psw2-chat","ord":"a0","title":"A tool call's row re-sends everything-so-far on every stream frame — the quadratic the prose path already fixed","todo":true,"desc":"Prose got an appends path precisely to avoid re-sending the sum of its prefixes; tool rows didn't. Each tool_call_update re-publishes the whole row — cumulative output and the full pretty-printed JSON of input+output — so a call producing X bytes over N frames puts ~X·N on the wire (transcript.ts:842-1051), and the JSON is re-stringified per frame before a deep-compare decides nothing changed (agent.ts:2100, 613).\n\nFix shape: an appends path for tool progress, and serialize the detail once when the call settles.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00"}
{"id":"perf-transcript-walks","parent":"psw2-chat","ord":"a1","title":"The panel re-walks the whole conversation on every tool frame and every turn","todo":true,"desc":"The open-question and armed-watcher counts iterate every row of the transcript ~3× per tool call (chat.ts:689-759), and the turn boundary walks all rows twice per turn (transcript.ts:637). Over a long agent session this is quadratic — the walks get longer as the conversation grows.\n\nFix shape: maintained counters and a remembered position, updated as rows change, instead of re-walking.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00"}
{"id":"perf-chat-code-rescan","parent":"psw2-chat","ord":"a2","title":"The browser re-scans every code span of a streaming message, every frame","todo":true,"desc":"The node-reference marker runs querySelectorAll(\"code\") over the whole growing message on each throttled frame and re-tests every span (chat/refs.ts:190, Entry.tsx:196) — quadratic in one message's length. The comment admits the pass and optimises only the writes.\n\nFix shape: scan only what the frame appended.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00"}
{"id":"psw2-web","parent":"perf-sweep-2026-08-24","ord":"a3","title":"Browser: page-sized leftovers (the vault walks really are gone)","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00"}
{"id":"perf-hide-done-copy","parent":"psw2-web","ord":"a0","title":"The \"hide done\" preference makes every write re-render the whole page","todo":true,"desc":"With the preference on, every arriving revision deep-copies every visible row (settings/done.ts:41 → derive.ts withoutDone's flatMap-spread). Spreading store proxies subscribes the memo to every field of every row and mints new objects for all of them — which defeats the keyed-merge fix, so one changed title re-runs ~25 bindings on every row. This is reactivity-after-the-flip's finding 2.11 come back through a different door.\n\nFix shape: hide without copying — filter by key, leave the row objects alone.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00"}
{"id":"perf-filter-title-cache","parent":"psw2-web","ord":"a1","title":"Filtering re-parses every matched title on every keystroke — the cache defeats itself","todo":true,"desc":"The rendered-title cache puts the search terms INSIDE its cache key, so each keystroke turns every matched markdown title into a cache miss and a full markdown parse (markdown/title.ts:134-143) — the exact cost the cache's own header says it exists to refuse. The misses also flood the shared 1024-entry cache, whose overflow clears EVERYTHING, evicting the whole app's titles. Same trigger re-computes every matched note's excerpt per keystroke (NodeBody.tsx:133).\n\nFix shape: cache the parse by title alone; apply match-highlighting as a cheap step outside the cached parse.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00"}
{"id":"perf-page-rescans","parent":"psw2-web","ord":"a2","title":"Smaller page-sized leftovers: drag, selection, caret, @-menu","todo":true,"desc":"Four milder ones, all bounded by the page or the file list rather than the vault: drag planning filters every placed row per pointer-move and runs 60×/s while the pointer parks at the autoscroll edge (drag/plan.ts:114, autoscroll.ts:157 — rows are y-sorted, a binary search is available); a held selection re-flattens the page on every revision for the whole duration of a bulk edit (select/selection.ts:150, admitted); an open caret costs one full flatten per arriving revision (edit/editing.tsx:287); and the chat @-menu scans every served path per keystroke (file/matching.ts:119).","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00"}
{"id":"psw2-ops","parent":"perf-sweep-2026-08-24","ord":"a4","title":"Ops: tool calls that touch everything","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00"}
{"id":"perf-capture-paths","parent":"psw2-ops","ord":"a0","title":"capture builds every node in the vault just to learn the list of filenames","todo":true,"desc":"Resolving where a capture lands calls the full outlines listing, which materializes and filters every node in the directory, only for the file paths to be kept (ops query.ts:967-1024, tools.ts:310) — paid per capture, sometimes twice via the retry wrapper.\n\nFix shape: a paths-only question; the listing keeps its counts for list_outlines, which genuinely needs them.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00"}
{"id":"perf-batch-assemble","parent":"psw2-ops","ord":"a1","title":"Each op in a batch re-assembles and re-sorts the whole directory","todo":true,"desc":"The batch fold rebuilds the full document set — fresh map, full path sort — once per op, so a 100-op batch pays it 100 times (following.ts:135, set.ts:152/304; the header admits it). Beside it, every write that names a file does a LINEAR find over all served paths (refusals.ts:207 via plan.ts:440 and friends), and the broken-files map is rebuilt at ~8 call sites per single write.\n\nFix shape: carry the assembled set and the derived maps forward across the batch; make path lookup a map hit.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00"}
{"id":"perf-homes-files","parent":"psw2-ops","ord":"a2","title":"Every fold click rebuilds two all-files structures — under a comment claiming it doesn't","todo":true,"desc":"homes builds a fresh Set of every outline path and a fresh broken-files Map per call (ops query.ts:533-534), and the browser calls it once per fold/unfold. The doc block above it asserts \"neither half is a walk\" — the id half is indeed a lookup; the file half is O(all files) per click.\n\nFix shape: hold both structures with the snapshot they describe; fix the comment either way.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00"}
{"id":"perf-didyoumean","parent":"psw2-ops","ord":"a3","title":"Every refused reference runs a similarity check against every id in the vault","todo":true,"desc":"\"Did you mean…\" walks all ids per unresolved reference — per refused edit from a stale tab, per bad id in a broken file, per armed chat chip (refusals.ts:75, suggest.ts:24, edit.ts, context.ts:81). Partly admitted; harmless one at a time, unbounded when a stale tab replays a burst of refusals.\n\nFix shape: cap the candidates (e.g. by length bucket) or compute suggestions lazily, only when a person will actually see them.","created":"2026-08-24T16:28:25-04:00"}
